ple  care like we actually care about the  stories that we're telling and how the  stories are told um i think probably  that we care less about what audiences  and critics feel about it than what we  feel about in our our sort of insular  circle of what kind of creative um spark  is between us as we are going through  our own individual processes um  probably that the priority for us is  on this set especially season two was  the health and well-being of the people  around us and i think people might be  surprised at how much  care is taken and not just the telling  of the story but in the storytellers  yeah one of the things also is you guys  started filming if i'm not mistaken uh  as right before covet hit and then you  went on that big break and i'm curious  did do you know um how did your  storyline possibly change as a result of  kovan being introduced into the story  you know  i i don't know if it changed all that  much i knew that mia was going to be  promoted to the executive producer job  um  but the ins and outs of how that was  going to translate itself  through season two i didn't know i think  that  the spark of inspiration as our  producers go through it the stories  always kind of shift and change from  from script to script and edit to edit  so you know you leave yourself flexible  as an actor to interpret whatever it is  they they consider to be the priority in  each episode um  but um i think for me during the  hiatus or the pause was  when we decided that we were going to  come back and tell a bit more of the  you know race and race politics after  having  lived through all of the social and  racial racial unrest in the united  states i i really wanted to link arms up  and and tell a really  nuanced um authentic story about what it  looks like to be  uh an african-american woman you know  empowered to do a job but you know  not really knowing how  to get the job done and not sure that  she could do it  with the hostility and the  microaggressions